\title{Self-Modifying Computation: Exact Simulation, Halting Equivalence, and the Limits of Semantic Monitoring}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 19, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Self-modifying programs can replace their own instructions during execution, suggesting a model stronger than ordinary fixed-program computation. We represent a configuration by a current program and a current data state, with transitions allowed to update both. Moving the mutable program into the state of a fixed interpreter gives an exact, step-preserving simulation. Conversely, every fixed program is a degenerate self-modifying machine with a singleton program component. The two halting problems are therefore mutually reducible, so effective self-modification is not strictly harder than classical computation in computability degree.

The equivalence transfers classical negative results. No total computable Boolean predictor decides termination for all program codes on a fixed input. Perpetual execution is exactly the complement of eventual halting, so an exact perpetual-safety monitor would decide halting. Perpetual execution at a fixed input is not recursively enumerable, precluding a complete effective finite-certificate discipline. Finally, a Rice-style argument excludes exact classifiers for every nontrivial extensional property of partial computable behavior, including semantic malware and unrestricted behavioral-alignment criteria. Bounded simulation remains decidable. Thus rewriting may alter resource costs and operational structure, but does not by itself create a higher degree of undecidability.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Self-modifying computation appears in polymorphic malware, dynamic binary translation, just-in-time compilation, reflective languages, genetic programming, and adaptive agents. Its characteristic operation is changing the program that determines subsequent state updates. Two claims are easily conflated. Termination of arbitrary universal self-modifying programs is undecidable, but self-modifying termination is not strictly harder than classical halting under ordinary effectiveness assumptions. A fixed interpreter can store changing code as data and simulate every rewrite.

This distinction matters for security and alignment. An exact semantic detector is not blocked specifically by syntactic mutation; it is blocked by the impossibility of deciding arbitrary nontrivial properties of computed behavior. Likewise, a monitor accepting exactly the systems that run safely forever would decide the complement of halting. Rewriting can make finite analysis difficult, while universal exactness was already impossible.

We develop the argument from first principles. We define the two machine models and effective reductions, prove exact simulations in both directions, establish the prediction and monitoring barriers, and derive semantic consequences. We also isolate a positive boundary: any fixed finite execution horizon can be checked directly.

\section{Definitions}
\begin{definition}[Self-modifying machine]
Let $P$ be a countable set of program representations and $S$ a countable set of ordinary states. A self-modifying configuration is $(p,s)\in P\times S$. A deterministic self-modifying machine is an effective partial transition
\[
\delta:P\times S\rightharpoonup P\times S.
\]
If $\delta(p,s)$ is undefined, execution halts. If $\delta(p,s)=(p',s')$, one step may change both program and state; in particular, $p'$ need not equal $p$.
\end{definition}

Starting from $c_0=(p_0,s_0)$, define $c_{n+1}=\delta(c_n)$ whenever the right-hand side is defined. Write $\operatorname{Halts}_{\delta}(c_0)$ when some finite stage has no successor.

\begin{definition}[Fixed-program machine]
A fixed-program machine on a countable state set $X$ is an effective partial transition $\tau:X\rightharpoonup X$. Its transition rule is immutable, although a state may contain an encoded program.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Many-one reduction]
For predicates $A$ on $X$ and $B$ on $Y$, a many-one reduction $A\le_m B$ is a total computable map $f:X\to Y$ such that $A(x)$ holds exactly when $B(f(x))$ holds. Predicate $A$ is strictly harder than $B$ when $B\le_m A$ but $A\not\le_m B$.
\end{definition}

Strict hardness concerns computability degree, not time, space, communication, or description length.

Fix an effective numbering $c\mapsto\varphi_c$ of partial computable maps $\mathbb N\rightharpoonup\mathbb N$.

\begin{definition}[Extensional nontrivial property]
A class $\mathcal C$ of partial functions is extensional because membership concerns the computed function rather than the syntax of a code. It is nontrivial among partial computable functions if there are partial computable $f,g$ with $f\in\mathcal C$ and $g\notin\mathcal C$.
\end{definition}

A predicate is recursively enumerable if an effective procedure eventually accepts exactly its positive instances. Equivalently, those instances can be effectively listed.

\section{Exact simulation and degree equivalence}
The central observation is that mutable code can be internalized as ordinary state.

\begin{theorem}[Exact fixed-state simulation]
For every effective self-modifying transition $\delta:P\times S\rightharpoonup P\times S$, there is a fixed-program machine on $P\times S$ whose run agrees with the self-modifying run at every finite stage. For every configuration $c$,
\[
\operatorname{Halts}_{\delta}(c)\iff \operatorname{Halts}_{\tau}(c).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Use a fixed interpreter whose data state stores $(p,s)$ and define $\tau(p,s)=\delta(p,s)$. At stage zero the states coincide. If they coincide at stage $n$, both apply the same transition and either halt together or reach the same successor. Induction gives step-for-step equality, including equality of finite halting stages.
\end{proof}

The construction places no bound on the number or content of rewrites. Newly generated code is simply the next stored value of $p$.

\begin{lemma}[Embedding fixed computation]
Every fixed-program transition $\tau:X\rightharpoonup X$ has a self-modifying representation with program set $\{\ast\}$ and state set $X$, defined by
\[
\delta(\ast,x)=(\ast,\tau(x))
\]
whenever $\tau(x)$ exists, and halting otherwise.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The only program value cannot change. Each transition performs exactly one step of $\tau$ on the state component, so induction gives equality of runs and halting behavior.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Mutual reducibility of halting]
The halting predicate of every effective self-modifying machine reduces to that of its fixed-state simulation. The simulator's halting predicate reduces to that of a singleton-program self-modifying machine. Hence self-modifying halting is not strictly harder than its fixed-program counterpart.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The forward reduction maps $(p,s)$ to the identical simulator state. The reverse maps simulator state $(p,s)$ to $(\ast,(p,s))$. The preceding results show that both computable maps preserve and reflect halting. The explicit forward reduction contradicts the missing-reduction condition required for strict hardness.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Allowing an unbounded effective number of code rewrites does not by itself produce a higher computability degree than ordinary halting.
\end{corollary}

This is an extensional conclusion. Interpretation can increase time or storage, while direct rewriting may specialize code efficiently. Quantitative hierarchies remain possible.

\section{Universal termination prediction}
\begin{theorem}[No general termination predictor]
For every fixed input $x\in\mathbb N$, there is no total computable Boolean function $D_x$ such that
\[
D_x(c)=1\iff \varphi_c(x)\text{ halts}
\]
for every program code $c$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $D_x$ existed, transform any program-input pair $(c,y)$ effectively into code for a program that ignores its own input and simulates $c$ on $y$. Querying $D_x$ would decide halting for arbitrary pairs. A diagonal program can then halt exactly when this alleged decider predicts that it does not halt on its own code. Either prediction contradicts its specified correctness.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No universal self-modifying predictor]
No total computable classifier decides halting for every configuration of a universal self-modifying model capable of representing fixed programs.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Compose such a classifier with the singleton-program embedding. The composition would contradict the preceding theorem.
\end{proof}

Unbounded prediction must be distinguished from finite exploration.

\begin{proposition}[Bounded halting]
Given an effective self-modifying transition, a configuration $c$, and $N\in\mathbb N$, whether the run halts within $N$ transitions is decidable.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Simulate at most $N$ steps. Accept on the first missing successor and reject after $N$ successful transitions. The loop is finite. If one transition costs $T_\delta$ and the largest stored configuration has size $M$, direct simulation uses $O(NT_\delta)$ time and $O(M)$ working space apart from trace output.
\end{proof}

\section{Perpetual safety and finite evidence}
\begin{definition}[Perpetual execution]
A configuration $c$ never halts when every finite stage of its run is defined:
\[
\operatorname{NeverHalts}_{\delta}(c)\iff
\forall n\in\mathbb N,\ \operatorname{run}_{\delta}(c,n)\text{ exists}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Complement identity]
For every $c$,
\[
\operatorname{NeverHalts}_{\delta}(c)
\iff \neg\operatorname{Halts}_{\delta}(c).
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Halting asserts the existence of a finite undefined stage. Negating that existential statement says every finite stage exists, exactly the definition of perpetual execution.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No exact perpetual-safety monitor]
Let $\delta$ be a self-modifying machine whose fixed-state simulation has undecidable halting. There is no total computable Boolean monitor $M$ satisfying
\[
M(c)=1\iff\operatorname{NeverHalts}_{\delta}(c)
\]
for every configuration $c$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $M$ existed, its Boolean negation would decide halting by the complement identity. Under exact simulation this would decide halting for the fixed-state machine, contradicting the hypothesis.
\end{proof}

A stronger barrier concerns positive recognition rather than total decision.

\begin{theorem}[Nonenumerability of perpetual execution]
For every fixed input $x$, the set
\[
\{c:\varphi_c(x)\text{ is undefined}\}
\]
is not recursively enumerable.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Halting at $x$ is recursively enumerable by direct simulation. If nonhalting at $x$ were also recursively enumerable, dovetail the two recognizers. Exactly one would eventually accept each code, yielding a total halting decider and contradicting the termination theorem.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No complete finite-certificate discipline]
There is no effective finite-certificate system with decidable certificate checking that is both sound and complete for perpetual execution in a universal model.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Enumerate all code-certificate pairs and output codes with valid certificates. Soundness and completeness would enumerate exactly the perpetual executions, contradicting nonenumerability.
\end{proof}

Sound but incomplete methods remain possible and useful. A type system, invariant discipline, or proof-carrying update mechanism may certify a broad fragment while necessarily abstaining on some genuinely perpetual executions.

\section{Nontrivial semantic properties}
\begin{theorem}[Semantic classification impossibility]
Let $\mathcal C$ be a set of partial functions $\mathbb N\rightharpoonup\mathbb N$. If some partial computable $f$ belongs to $\mathcal C$ and some partial computable $g$ does not, then no total computable classifier $D$ satisfies
\[
D(c)=1\iff\varphi_c\in\mathcal C
\]
for every code $c$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Choose computable behaviors on opposite sides of $\mathcal C$. From a program-input pair with unknown halting behavior, effectively construct a program whose extensional behavior falls on one chosen side or the other according to whether that computation halts. An exact classifier would distinguish those cases and decide halting. This is the Rice reduction; extensionality ensures that only computed behavior matters, and nontriviality supplies the two distinguishable behaviors.
\end{proof}

Both assumptions matter. A property containing all partial computable functions, or none, has a constant decider. A syntactic property such as containing a chosen byte sequence need not be extensional and may be decidable.

\begin{corollary}[Semantic malware detection]
Let $\mathcal M$ be a nontrivial extensional notion of malicious partial behavior. No total computable procedure classifies every program exactly according to $\mathcal M$.
\end{corollary}

Polymorphism makes signatures easier to evade, but this result is stronger: even perfect access to program text does not produce an exact semantic classifier for arbitrary universal programs. Practical systems must employ restrictions, bounded analysis, syntactic approximations, probabilistic judgments, or incomplete semantic methods.

\begin{corollary}[Behavioral alignment under self-revision]
If an alignment criterion $\mathcal A$ is a nontrivial extensional property of partial computable behavior, no total computable algorithm decides for every adaptive program whether its complete behavior belongs to $\mathcal A$.
\end{corollary}

The corollary does not prohibit alignment methods. It identifies an impossible conjunction: unrestricted universal programs, a nontrivial extensional requirement, total evaluator termination, and exact soundness and completeness. Restricting a language, accepting abstention, checking finite horizons, or using conservative certificates can recover useful guarantees.

\section{Algorithms and finite demonstrations}
The simulation algorithm stores $(p_0,s_0)$, repeatedly applies the fixed interpreter for $\delta$, reports halting if no successor exists, and otherwise stores the successor pair. After $N$ steps its state exactly equals the self-modifying configuration after $N$ steps.

For a numerical illustration, let mutable code be two integers $(a,b)$ and ordinary state be $z$. While $z$ is below a threshold, execute
\[
z'=z+a,\qquad (a',b')=(b,a+b).
\]
Starting from $(a,b,z)=(1,1,0)$, successive increments are Fibonacci numbers $1,1,2,3,5,8,\ldots$. A fixed interpreter storing the triple $(a,b,z)$ produces the identical trace. The distinction is solely whether $(a,b)$ is called program text or data.

A finite-horizon monitor runs at most $N$ transitions and truthfully reports either ``halt observed'' or ``survived $N$ steps.'' The latter is not a certificate of perpetual execution. For any selected horizon $N$, a counter program that halts at time $N+1$ has the same first $N$ states as one that counts forever. Thus longer observation increases finite evidence but never converts a finite prefix into a universal guarantee.

\section{Applications}
\subsection{Security architecture}
The semantic theorem supports layered defense. Signatures address decidable syntax; sandboxes restrict effects; bounded emulation detects behavior within a budget; certificate systems recognize selected enumerable fragments; statistical models exchange exactness for empirical reach. None should be specified as a total exact solver for arbitrary nontrivial semantics.

\subsection{Adaptive systems}
A system may require each self-update to carry a checkable local safety certificate. The nonenumerability theorem implies that an effective finite-certificate regime cannot recognize every safe universal computation. Consequently, abstention is mathematically necessary for sound general monitoring. ``Not certified'' must remain distinct from ``demonstrated unsafe.''

\subsection{Semantics and runtime design}
The simulation theorem justifies representing code heaps, generated procedures, and mutable policies as explicit state under one fixed transition relation. This representation preserves every finite execution and is suitable for bounded exploration and trace comparison, though it may incur large resource overhead.

\section{Limitations and future work}
The equivalence assumes that programs and states are finitely representable and every transition is effective. Noncomputable oracles, infinite precision, or noneffective transition rules can change computational power; rewriting alone does not.

Mutual reducibility does not imply equal complexity. Machines with at most $k$ and $k+1$ rewrites may exhibit strict differences in optimal time, space, communication, or description length despite computing the same functions. Developing such resource-bounded rewrite hierarchies is a natural next step.

Oracle-relative models should preserve the simulation pattern: with oracle $A$, mutable code remains state while oracle access supplies the extra power, suggesting halting degree $A'$ rather than another jump from rewriting.

Monitoring can also be studied quantitatively. Nonenumerability suggests infinite structured blind spots for any total sound certifier, possibly even among systems with bounded rewrite rate. Distributional semantic detection offers another direction: under computable full-support distributions, one can seek error lower bounds governed by entropy or complexity assumptions. Finally, proof-carrying alignment invites characterization of large recursively enumerable fragments admitting finite certificates that all reachable rewrites preserve a chosen property.

The perpetual-safety model used here identifies safety with nonhalting. Rich temporal properties require a reduction from halting or perpetual execution before the same conclusion applies. Likewise, semantic impossibility concerns extensional properties and worst cases; syntactic policies, restricted domains, and distributional performance remain separate questions.

\section{Conclusion}
Effective self-modification is operationally dramatic but computability-theoretically conservative. A fixed interpreter stores mutable code alongside state and reproduces every transition; ordinary computation embeds back as the case of a singleton program. Their halting predicates are mutually reducible.

The classical barriers remain. Universal termination prediction is impossible. Perpetual execution is the complement of halting and admits neither an exact total monitor nor a complete effective enumeration. Every nontrivial extensional property of partial computable behavior defeats exact classification, encompassing semantic malware and unrestricted behavioral alignment criteria.

These limits define a constructive boundary. Bounded simulation, restricted languages, conservative certificates, syntactic controls, and probabilistic methods remain available. What cannot exist is a universal algorithm that is simultaneously total, exact, and complete about the unbounded semantic future of arbitrary code.

