# Explore Self-Modifying Computation

A program that rewrites itself looks as if it changes the rules mid-game. The central insight is subtler: a fixed interpreter can carry the current code as data, reproducing every transition without changing its own instructions.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

Use the trace explorer first, then inspect the exact simulation algorithm.

{{algorithm:0}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the exact simulation argument</summary>

Store the mutable configuration $(p,s)$ as the ordinary state of a fixed interpreter. Both presentations begin at the same pair. If their states agree before one transition, both apply the same effective transition and therefore halt together or produce the same successor. Induction gives equality at every finite stage.

</details>

## What finite observation can establish

Bounded simulation is decisive about a bounded question: did a halt occur within the selected horizon? It cannot certify an infinite future.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{demo:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal why exact perpetual monitoring is impossible</summary>

Perpetual execution is the logical complement of eventual halting. If a total exact monitor recognized all and only perpetual runs, negating its Boolean answer would decide halting. For universal computation, that contradicts diagonalization.

</details>

## The adversary beyond the horizon

For every finite horizon, one machine can imitate a perpetual counter throughout the visible prefix and halt immediately afterward.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{demo:2}}

{{visualization:0}}

## From termination to semantic behavior

The obstacle extends to every nontrivial property determined solely by the partial function a program computes. This is why exact universal semantic malware detection and exact unrestricted behavioral-alignment classification cannot be total computable procedures.

{{algorithm:2}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the semantic reduction</summary>

Choose one computable behavior inside the property and another outside it. Arrange a new program's behavior to fall on opposite sides depending on whether a selected computation halts. An exact classifier would distinguish the cases and decide halting.

</details>

## A constructive design boundary

Useful analysis remains possible through restricted languages, finite horizons, conservative certificates, sandboxing, and statistical methods. The fixed-state bridge helps implement such analyses without claiming an impossible universal oracle.

